Introduction
	XFaceMaker is the only UIMS which has the capability
	of generating true widget classes conforming to the
	programming interface of the X Toolkit Intrinsics.
	
	Given an object edited with XFaceMaker, you can generate
	a widget class which has the look and behaviour of this
	object built into it.
	
	The new widget class can be instanciated immediately:
	XFaceMaker creates the class dynamically and adds it to
	its Toolkit icons. You can use your new class just like
	any standard Motif class.
	
	You can also generate the C code for your new widget
	class: XFaceMaker will produce the Class.c, Class.h and
	ClassP.h files automatically.
	
	In your widget class, you can define not only different
	default values for existing Motif resources, but also
	completely new resources. You specify the side-effects
	of these new resources using XFaceMaker active values
	and FACE scripts.
	
Creating a Widget Class
	To create a simple widget class, you must first create the
	object which will be used as the model for the class.
	The model object is created like any ordinary interface object.
	It can be a single widget, or a composite widget with sub-widgets.
	
	Once the model object is defined, you must select it and call the
	command `Modules/Classes/Save As' in the main menu bar of XFaceMaker.
	This will pop up a special purpose file selector in which you must
	specify:
	
	- the name of the class,
	
	- the .fm file where the class definition will be saved.
	
	When you click `OK', the selected object is saved in the specified .fm
	file, and the widget class is created dynamically by XFaceMaker.
	An icon is created in the toolkit area of
	the main window, in the `User defined' section: to see it, use the Toolkit
	pulldown menu and select `User defined'. (Note: if you specified a class
	name starting with `Xnsl', the new class appears in the `NSL'
	section of the toolkit).
	
	You can also load a class that was defined previously. To do this,
	use the command `Modules/Classes/Load'. The class selector pops
	up and lets you choose the class name and file. The class model is loaded,
	the class is created dynamically and the icon is added to the Toolkit.
	
	To generate the C code for the new class:
	
	- Open the C Code Options box by `Options/C Code' and check the `Save C Also'
	toggle,
	
	- Save your class again (select the model object and use
	`Modules/Classes/Save'). The .c and .h files are generated.
	
	Notes:
	
	1. The `File/Save Prefs' command records the classes that
	you have saved or loaded, so you don't have to load them again when
	you restart XFaceMaker. Loaded classes are also recorded in project
	files so they are created automatically when the project file is
	loaded.
	
	2. Widget classes are NOT loaded automatically when you load an
	interface containing class instances. You must load classes explicitly
	or record them in your .xfm.startup or in your project file.
	
Changing Default Resource Values
	In the simplest cases, you define widget classes to specify
	special resource values for existing Motif resources. For example,
	you can define a very simple class `OKButton' as follows:
	
	- create a PushButton and select it,
	
	- change its `labelString' resource to `OK',
	
	- save it as a new widget class `OKButton' in a file `OKButton.fm'.
	
	When you save the class, XFaceMaker creates a widget class which
	is a sub-class of XmPushButton, and which defines the resource
	`labelString' with a default value of `OK'. You can save the C code
	files of your class as explained in `Creating a Widget Class' and have
	a look at the resource array and class record definition.
	
	You can specify not only the look for the new class, but more usefully
	its behavior by defining callbacks. For example, you could program
	the activateCallback of your PushButton (say, ``printf("OK\n");'').
	In that case, the callback will be built into the new class (of course,
	it is still possible to define additional callbacks in the class instance).
	
Defining New Resources
	In addition to specifying new default values for existing resources,
	you can define completely new resources in your new widget class.
	
	To define a new resource:
	
	- Select your model object.
	
	- Open the Active Values box (`Windows/Active Values' command).
	
	- Select the usage toggle `Widget Class'.
	
	- Enter the name of your new resource in the `Name' text field.
	
	- Enter the type of the resource in the `Type' field (you can choose
	an existing type using the `...' button, or enter a new type -- see
	`Defining New Resource Types').
	
	- Enter the resource's default value (or leave it blank, in which
	case the default value will be 0).
	
	- In the `Get Script' text field, you can enter a FACE script which
	will be executed when the resource is retrieved from the class instance
	using XtGetValues. The value which will be returned by XtGetValues
	must stored in the @ argument of the FACE script.
	This script can be left blank in which case no side
	effect will be executed. 
	
	- In the `Set Script' text field, you can enter a FACE script which
	will be executed when the resource is changed in the class instance
	using XtSetValues, and also when the resource is initialized when
	the class instance is created. The new value which was passed to
	XtSetValues is stored in the @ argument of the FACE script, and
	can be used to update the widget accordingly. The previous value of
	the resource is also passed as @1. To know if the script is called
	at creation time or by an XtGetValues, you can test the value
	@2 which is null at creation time and non-null at SetValues time.
	This script can be left blank in which case no side effect will be
	executed.
	
	- Click on the `Add' button of the Active Values box.
	
	- Save your class (you can use the toplevel menu's `Modules/Classes/Save As',
	or enter the class name in the `Class Name' field of the Active Values box
	and click on the `Save' button).
	
Defining New Resource Types
	When you define a new resource, you must specify its type.
	
	There are basically 3 cases.
	
	
	1. Your resource can be represented by an existing Motif or Xt type.
	This is the case for Strings, XmStrings, Booleans, integers, etc.
	In that case, all you have to do it choose the right type from the
	list. When possible, we recommend that you use existing representation
	types.
	
	
	2. You resource cannot be represented by an existing type, but it is
	a scalar resource which will accept only a fixed set of values, i.e.
	an enumerated type. In that case, XFaceMaker provides a simple means
	of declaring the type:
	
	- in the Type field, enter the name of your new type (say, `MyType');
	
	- in the Set Script of your resource, declare the type and its possible
	values as follows:
	
	type MyType my_value_1, my_value_2, my_value_2;
	
	(the declaration can be placed anywhere in the Set script, but before
	any call to the type caster MyType(...));
	
	XFaceMaker handles all the declarations and code necessary to define
	new enumerated types. You do not have anything more to do.
	
	
	3. Your resource hold data that cannot be represented by a standard
	Motif or Xt representation, and is not an enumeration. In that case,
	you only enter the name of the new type in the `Type' field, but you
	will not be able to manipulate it through FACE scripts in the standard
	version of XFaceMaker. You have to write C code to convert and manipulate
	your new type, and add it to an extended version of XFaceMaker. See
	the documentation for details.
	
Defining Class Methods
	When you define a new widget class, you can program the Core and Composite
	class methods of your class.
	
	To program a class method:
	
	- Click on the `Methods' button in the Active Values box;
	
	- Select the method you want to program;
	
	- Enter the FACE script that will be executed when the class method
	is called in Set Script area;
	
	See the documentation for a list of methods that can be programmed in
	this way, and for explanations on the arguments passed to the corresponding
	FACE script.
